ys in `nextGeneration` lack context.\n\nLet me think about what the cleanest, most intent-revealing version would be.\n\nOption A — minimal rename:\n```typescript\nconst sameCells = (a: Cell[], b: Cell[]): boolean => {\n  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;\n  const cellKey = (c: Cell) => `${c[0]},${c[1]}`;\n  const bKeys = new Set(b.map(cellKey));\n  return a.every((c) => bKeys.has(cellKey(c)));\n};\n```\n\nThis improves `key` → `cellKey` and `bs` → `bKeys`. Both clearer.\n\nShould I also extract magic arrays? Let me weigh. The arrays `[[0,0],[1,0],[0,1]]` and `[[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]]` represent specific Game of Life patterns. Naming them would help:\n\n```typescript\nconst L_SHAPE: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]];\nconst TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]];\n```\n\nThen:\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE)) {\n    return TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nThis significantly improves intent (Rule 2). The reader now understands these are the L-shape and the 2x2 block, not random coordinates.\n\nHowever, this adds 2 bindings (Rule 4 — fewest elements). But Rule 2 > Rule 4, so intent wins.\n\nHmm, but there's a tension. This is a hardcoded special case that will likely be replaced. Is naming it \"L_SHAPE\" creating a premature abstraction? \n\nActually, no. The names describe what the values ARE (an L-shape pattern and a 2x2 block pattern), not how they're used. These are factual descriptions. Even if the implementation later becomes general, removing these constants is trivial.\n\nLet me consider the APP mass impact.\n\nActually, let me think about whether extracting constants actually reduces or increases mass.\n\nCurrent `nextGeneration`:\n- `if (sameCells(cells, [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]]))` — 6 constants in the array\n- `return [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]];` — 8 constants\n- `return [];` — 1 constant\n\nWith extraction:\n- `const L_SHAPE: Cell[] = 