) = 2\n\nSum: 2+2+2+3+3+3+3+2+2+2 = 24\n\nSo bindings went up by 4 (from the 2 new constant definitions + their usages, net). Actually let me recount. The extraction removed the inline arrays (which were constants, not bindings) and replaced with binding references. \n\nConstants before: the inline arrays contributed 6+8 = 14 constants.\nConstants after: the arrays moved to const definitions, still 6+8 = 14 constants. Same.\n\nBut after, we have 2 new bindings (L_SHAPE, TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK defs) + their 2 usages replacing... nothing (they're new references). So bindings +4 roughly, but constants unchanged.\n\nHmm wait. Before, in nextGeneration:\n- `sameCells(cells, [[0,0],[1,0],[0,1]])` — `cells` binding + 6 constants\n- `return [[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]]` — 8 constants\n- `return []` — 1 constant\n\nAfter, in nextGeneration:\n- `sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE)` — `cells` binding + `L_SHAPE` binding\n- `return TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` — `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` binding\n- `return []` — 1 constant\n\nSo in nextGeneration: before had 14 constants + 1 (cells, already counted). After has 2 bindings (L_SHAPE, TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK usages) + 1 constant ([]). The 14 constants moved to module-level const definitions where they're still 14 constants + 2 new assignments (const L_SHAPE =, const TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK =) + 2 new bindings (the names).\n\nSo net change:\n- Constants: +0 (14 moved, same count)\n- Bindings: +4 (L_SHAPE def+use, TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK def+use)... but actually 2 of these replace inline, so the \"use\" bindings are new references. Net +4 binding occurrences? Let me think: before nextGeneration used `cells` (2 occurrences). After uses `cells`(2) + `L_SHAPE`(1) + `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK`(1) = +2 binding occurrences in nextGeneration, plus module level L_SHAPE def + TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK def = +2. Total +4.\n- Assignments: +2 (two new const definitions) × 6 = +12\n- Everything else same.\n\nSo mass change ≈ +4 (bindings) + 12 (assignments) = +16. Mass increases.\n\nHmm. So the constant extraction INCREA