e (1)\n\n`  const key = (c) => \\`${c[0]},${c[1]}\\`;`\n- Binding: key(1), c(1), c[0](1), c[1](1) = 4\n- Constant: \",\" (1)\n- Assignment: const key = (6)\n\n`  const bs = new Set(b.map(key));`\n- Binding: bs(1), b(1), key(1) = 3\n- Invocation: new Set(2), b.map(2) = 4\n- Loop: b.map (5)\n- Assignment: const bs = (6)\n\n`  return a.every((c) => bs.has(key(c)));`\n- Binding: a(1), c(1), bs(1), key(1), c(1) = 5  [a.every, callback c, bs.has, key, key's arg c]\n- Invocation: a.every(2), bs.has(2), key(2) = 6\n- Loop: a.every (5)\n\n`export function nextGeneration(cells) {`\n- Binding: cells(1)\n\n`  if (sameCells(cells, [[0,0],[1,0],[0,1]])) {`\n- Conditional: if (4)\n- Binding: sameCells(1), cells(1) = 2\n- Invocation: sameCells(2)\n- Constant: 0,0,1,0,0,1 = 6\n\n`    return [[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]];`\n- Constant: 0,0,1,0,0,1,1,1 = 8\n\n`  }`\n`  return [];`\n- Constant: [] (1)\n\nLet me sum current:\n- Constants: 1 (false) + 1 (\",\") + 6 (L array) + 8 (block array) + 1 ([]) = 17\n- Bindings: 3 + 2 + 4 + 3 + 5 + 1 + 2 = 20\n- Invocations: 4 + 6 + 2 = 12\n- Conditionals: 4 + 4 = 8\n- Loops: 5 + 5 = 10\n- Assignments: 6 + 6 + 6 = 18\n\nTotal current = 17 + 20 + 12 + 8 + 10 + 18 = 85\n\n**REFACTORED:**\n\n`const L_SHAPE: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]];`\n- Binding: L_SHAPE(1)\n- Constant: 0,0,1,0,0,1 = 6\n- Assignment: const L_SHAPE = (6)\n\n`const TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]];`\n- Binding: TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK(1)\n- Constant: 0,0,1,0,0,1,1,1 = 8\n- Assignment: const TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK = (6)\n\n`const sameCells = (a, b) => {`\n- Binding: sameCells(1), a(1), b(1) = 3\n- Assignment: (6)\n\n`  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;`\n- Conditional: (4)\n- Binding: a(1), b(1) = 2\n- Constant: false (1)\n\n`  const cellKey = (c) => \\`${c[0]},${c[1]}\\`;`\n- Binding: cellKey(1), c(1), c[0](1), c[1](1) = 4\n- Constant: \",\" (1)\n- Assignment: (6)\n\n`  const bKeys = new Set(b.map(cellKey));`\n- Binding: bKeys(1), b(1), cellKey(1) = 3\n- Invocation: new Set(2), b.map(2) = 4\n- Loop: (5)\n- Assignment: (6)\n\n`  return a.every((c) => bKeys.has(cellKey(c)));`\n- Binding: a(1), c(1), bKeys(1), cellKey(1), c(1) = 5\n- Invocation: a.every(2), bKeys.has(2), cellKey(2) = 6\n- Loop: (5)\n\n`export function nextGeneration(cells) {`\n- Binding: cells(1)\n\n`  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE)) {`\n- Conditional: (4)\n- Binding: sameCells(1), cells(1), L_SHAPE(1) = 3\n- Invocation: sameCells(2)\n\n`    return TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK;`\n- Binding: TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK(1)\n\n`  }`\n`  return [];`\n- Constant: [] (1)\n\nSum refactored:\n- Constants: 6 (L_SHAPE) + 8 (block) + 1 (false) + 1 (\",\") + 1 ([]) = 17\n- Bindings: 1 + 1 + 3 + 2 + 4 + 3 + 5 + 1 + 3 + 1 = 24\n- Invocations: 4 + 6 + 2 = 12\n- Conditionals: 4 + 4 = 8\n- Loops: 5 + 5 = 10\n- Assignments: 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 + 6 = 30\n\nWait let me recount assignments. Current assignments: const sameCells, const key, const bs = 3 × 6 = 18.\nRefactored assignments: const L_SHAPE, const TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK, const sameCells, const cellKey, const bKeys = 5 × 6 = 30.\n\nTotal refactored = 17 + 24 + 12 + 8 + 10 + 30 = 101\n\nDelta = 101 - 85 = +16.\n\nSo mass increases by 16 due to the constant extraction (2 new assignments = 12, plus 4 new binding occurrences = 4, total +16). The renames are mass-neutral.\n\nHmm. +16 mass for the clarity of named patterns. \n\nPer the rules: \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass.\" and \"Don't sacrifice readability for mass.\" So this is justified IF the clarity gain is real.\n\nIs it real? Going from `[[0,0],[1,0],[0,1]]` to `L_SHAPE` — yes, meaningful. The reader of `nextGeneration` immediately sees \"if L-shape, return 2x2 block\" without parsing coordinates.\n\nOK. But I'm a little worried about over-investing in hardcoded fakes. Let me reconsider a middle ground: do ONLY the renames (mass-neutral, pure clarity), and skip the constant extraction (which costs +16 mass and names temporary fakes).\n\nThe mandate is \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring.\" The renames satisfy that. They're clean, safe, zero-mass intent improvements.\n\nHmm, but the renames alone (`bs`→`bKeys`, `key`→`cellKey`) are fairly minor. Are they \"enough\" of a refactoring? They ARE legitimate Rule 2 improvements. `bs` is genuinely cryptic. Renaming it is a real clarity win.\n\nLet me make a final decision. I'll do the renames AND the constant extraction, because together they form a coherent \"reveal intent\" refactoring that makes both `sameCells` and `nextGeneration` read clearly. The +16 mass is an acceptable trade for clarity per Rule 2 > APP.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider one more time, because I want to make the right call, not just a defensible one.\n\nThe hardcoded fake: `nextGeneration` returns a hardcoded block for the L-shape input. This is a known TDD stepping stone. The NEXT tests (2x2 block unchanged, blinker) will force a generalization. When that happens, `L_SHAPE` and `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` constants and `sameCells` will likely be deleted entirely (replaced by neighbor-counting logic).\n\nSo the question: is it worth naming constants that are about to be deleted?\n\nArguments:\n- Pro: Right NOW, the code is clearer. The next reader (which might be me in the next Green phase) benefits. Refactoring is about the current state, not the future.\n- Con: It's polishing throwaway code. Mass goes up for something that'll be deleted.\n\nThe TDD refactoring principle: \"Refactor after each green, to keep code clean as you go.\" Even if code will change, keeping it clean now helps you see the next step. So I lean pro.\n\nBut there's also the principle of not over-engineering. Naming 2 constants isn't over-engineering — it's just clarity.\n\nOK, I've decided. Final plan:\n1. Rename `key` → `cellKey`\n2. Rename `bs` → `bKeys`  \n3. Extract `L_SHAPE` and `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` constants\n\nThis is one cohesive \"Reveal Intent\" refactoring (Rule 2). Mass +16, justified by clarity.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider whether I should be more conservative. The mass increase of 16 is notable. And honestly, the renames alone are a perfectly good, clean refactoring that's mass-neutral. \n\nYou know what, I think the most defensible and clean choice is: do the renames (zero mass, clear win) PLUS the constant extraction (clarity win, +16 mass accepted per Rule 2). Both improve intent. I'll document the trade-off clearly.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider the constant extraction's value vs cost one final time, because +16 mass is real and the guideline says \"Context matters: Don't sacrifice readability for mass\" — implying usually you DON'T want to increase mass unless readability truly demands it.\n\nDoes readability truly demand naming these arrays? In `nextGeneration`:\n```typescript\nif (sameCells(cells, [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]])) {\n  return [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]];\n}\n```\nA reader sees: \"if cells equal this specific set of 3 coordinates, return this specific set of 4 coordinates.\" They have to parse coordinates to realize it's an L-shape becoming a block. The test description tells us it's an \"L-shape → block\" transformation. The code doesn't communicate that.\n\nWith named constants, the code directly states the transformation. That's a genuine readability improvement.\n\nI'll go with it. Final.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider if there's an even better refactoring I'm missing.\n\nIs there duplication? The `sameCells` function builds a Set from `b` then checks all of `a`. This is correct set-equality. No duplication.\n\nCould `nextGeneration` be simplified? It's already minimal for the current tests.\n\nCould I remove `sameCells` and inline? Inlining would be: \n```typescript\nif (cells.length === 3 && ...) // messy\n```\nNo, `sameCells` is clearer. Keep.\n\nWhat about the empty-array and small-array cases returning `[]`? They fall through to the final `return []`. That's fine — the default. Good.\n\nOK so the refactoring is the renames + constant extraction. Let me also reconsider: is `sameCells` even needed if I extract constants? Yes — `sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE)` still needs `sameCells` to do order-independent comparison. Keep it.\n\nAlright, let me also double-check the renames don't break anything. `cellKey` and `bKeys` are local to `sameCells`. Renaming is safe. The constants are module-level, used in `nextGeneration`. Safe.\n\nLet me write the refactored file.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the constant naming for `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK`. Alternatives: `BLOCK`, `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK`, `BLOCK_2X2`. The GoL canonical term is \"block\" (a 2x2 still life). The test says \"2x2 block\". I think `BLOCK` is the cleanest domain term and any GoL reader knows \"block\" = 2x2 still life. But `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` is more self-explanatory for non-GoL readers and matches the test's \"2x2 block\" phrasing exactly.\n\nGiven Rule 2 (reveal intent to any reader), `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` is safer. But it's verbose. Hmm. \n\nThe test uses \"2x2 block\". To match domain language of the spec, `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` aligns. I'll use `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK`.\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider. `L_SHAPE` and `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` — inconsistent: one is a shape name, the other is a size+name. For consistency maybe `L_SHAPE` and `BLOCK` (both pattern names). Or `L_TRIOMINO`... no. \n\nIn GoL: \"block\" is the 2x2 still life. \"L-shape\" / the test's L is actually 3 cells forming an L (a triomino). The transformation L → block happens because the L's 3 cells give the 4th corner exactly 3 neighbors (birth).\n\nI'll name them to match the spec's language:\n- Input: `L_SHAPE` (test says \"L-shape\")\n- Output: `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` (test says \"2x2 block\")\n\nThis directly maps code to spec terminology. Good for Rule 2.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — maybe just `BLOCK` since the spec also just says \"block\" multiple times (\"returns the 2x2 block\", \"block [(0,0)...]\"). The dominant term is \"block\". I'll use `BLOCK` for conciseness. Hmm, but the very next todo test is \"2x2 block unchanged\" — there's only one block pattern in GoL so `BLOCK` is unambiguous.\n\nUgh, I'm overthinking the name. Let me go with `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` to be maximally self-documenting (Rule 2 — reveal intent to ANY reader, even one unfamiliar with GoL terminology). Final.\n\nWait, no. Let me reconsider. `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` is awkward to read. `BLOCK` is the canonical GoL term. A reader unfamiliar with GoL can still see `BLOCK` and the array `[[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]]` and understand it's a 2x2 block of cells. The constant name + value together are clear.\n\nI'll use `BLOCK`. It's clean, canonical, and the value makes the \"2x2\" obvious.\n\nHmm, but then `L_SHAPE` and `BLOCK` — the input is named by its shape, the output by its GoL name. Slight inconsistency but both are clear pattern names. Fine.\n\nOK FINAL decision: `L_SHAPE` and `BLOCK`. Renames: `cellKey`, `bKeys`.\n\nHmm, wait, let me reconsider `BLOCK` vs `TWO_BY_TWO_BLOCK` based on the test wording once more. The test: \"L-shape [(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)] -> block [(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)]\". So the test calls the input \"L-shape\" and output \"block\". So `L_SHAPE` and `BLOCK` perfectly mirror the test's own terms! Great, that settles it. `L_SHAPE` and `BLOCK`.\n\nLet me write it.\n\n```typescript\nexport type Cell = [number, number];\n\nconst L_SHAPE: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1]];\nconst BLOCK: Cell[] = [[0, 0], [1, 0], [0, 1], [1, 1]];\n\nconst sameCells = (a: Cell[], b: Cell[]): boolean => {\n  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;\n  const cellKey = (c: Cell) => `${c[0]},${c[1]}`;\n  const bKeys = new Set(b.map(cellKey));\n  return a.every((c) => bKeys.has(cellKey(c)));\n};\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE)) {\n    return BLOCK;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nNow `nextGeneration` reads: \"If the cells are the L-shape, return the block; otherwise nothing.\" That's clear and intent-revealing.\n\nLet me verify tests still pass after writing.\n\nLet me al