etitive. Each case follows the same pattern: check if input matches a known shape, return the known successor.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication (DRY)\n\nThere's clear duplication here. The pattern `if (sameCells(cells, X)) return Y;` is repeated three times (with the first two OR'd together). \n\nWe could introduce a lookup table mapping known patterns to their successors. This would:\n- Remove the repeated `sameCells` invocation pattern\n- Make the data-driven structure clearer\n- Reduce the conditional logic\n\nLet me think about this. A lookup table approach:\n\n```typescript\nconst SUCCESSORS: Array<[Cell[], Cell[]]> = [\n  [L_SHAPE, BLOCK],\n  [BLOCK, BLOCK],\n  [VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER],\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  for (const [pattern, successor] of SUCCESSORS) {\n    if (sameCells(cells, pattern)) {\n      return successor;\n    }\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nWait, but this introduces a loop. Let me think about whether this is actually better.\n\nPros:\n- Eliminates the duplicated `sameCells(cells, X)` invocation pattern\n- Adds new cases by just adding to the table (data-driven)\n- Clearer intent: \"find the successor for this pattern\"\n\nCons:\n- Introduces a loop (mass 5)\n- But removes 2 conditionals and consolidates the OR\n\nActually, let me think about using `find`:\n\n```typescript\nconst SUCCESSORS: Array<[Cell[], Cell[]]> = [\n  [L_SHAPE, BLOCK],\n  [BLOCK, BLOCK],\n  [VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER],\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const match = SUCCESSORS.find(([pattern]) => sameCells(cells, pattern));\n  return match ? match[1] : [];\n}\n```\n\nThis uses `find` (a loop, mass 5) and a ternary (conditional, mass 4).\n\nHmm, let me compare masses.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The DRY principle here is about the conceptual duplication: the \"lookup a known pattern\" concept is repeated. The lookup table approach centralizes this knowledge.\n\nBut wait — is this the right time to refactor? We only have 3 cases, and the next tests are:\n- overpopulation (plus shape → ring)\n- negative coordinates\n- full oscillator cycle\n\nThese upcoming tests will likely require a GENERAL algorithm, not more hardcoded cases. So the lookup table might be premature — the next tests may force a complete rewrite to the actual Game of Life rules.\n\nHowever, the refactoring rules say I MUST attempt at least one refactoring. Let me think about what's the best refactoring that:\n1. Keeps tests green\n2. Reduces duplication (Rule 3)\n3. Doesn't over-engineer (Rule 4)\n\nGiven that the next tests will likely need a general algorithm, I should be careful not to over-invest in the hardcoded-cases structure. But there IS clear duplication right now that I can address.\n\nLet me reconsider. The three cases each have the pattern `sameCells(cells, PATTERN) → SUCCESSOR`. The lookup table eliminates this duplication cleanly.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether a lookup table is over-engineering for 3 entries. With only 3 entries, the explicit if-statements are arguably clearer (Rule 2). But the lookup table is also clear and more easily extensible.\n\nLet me think about the trade-off more carefully using APP mass.\n\nLet me calculate the current mass carefully.\n\nCurrent implementation:\n\n```typescript\nconst sameCells = (a: Cell[], b: Cell[]): boolean => {\n  if (a.length !== b.length) return false;\n  const cellKey = (c: Cell) => `${c[0]},${c[1]}`;\n  const bKeys = new Set(b.map(cellKey));\n  return a.every((c) => bKeys.has(cellKey(c)));\n};\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE) || sameCells(cells, BLOCK)) {\n    return BLOCK;\n  }\n  if (sameCells(cells, VERTICAL_BLINKER)) {\n    return HORIZONTAL_BLINKER;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nLet me count the `nextGeneration` function only (the part being refactored), since `sameCells` and the constants stay the same.\n\nActually, the constants and `sameCells` don't change in my refactoring, so let me focus the mass comparison on `nextGeneration`.\n\n**Current `nextGeneration` mass:**\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE) || sameCells(cells, BLOCK)) {\n    return BLOCK;\n  }\n  if (sameCells(cells, VERTICAL_BLINKER)) {\n    return HORIZONTAL_BLINKER;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\n- Bindings: `cells` (parameter) = 1\n- Constants: `[]` (empty array return) = 1\n- Invocations: `sameCells` × 3 = 6\n- Conditionals: \n  - first `if` = 4\n  - `||` = 4\n  - second `if` = 4\n  - total = 12\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0\n\nTotal: 1 + 1 + 6 + 12 = 20\n\n**Refactored `nextGeneration` with lookup table:**\n\n```typescript\nconst SUCCESSORS: Array<[Cell[], Cell[]]> = [\n  [L_SHAPE, BLOCK],\n  [BLOCK, BLOCK],\n  [VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER],\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const match = SUCCESSORS.find(([pattern]) => sameCells(cells, pattern));\n  return match ? match[1] : [];\n}\n```\n\nFor `nextGeneration`:\n- Bindings: `cells` (param), `match` = 2\n- Constants: `[]` = 1\n- Invocations: `SUCCESSORS.find`, `sameCells` = 4 (well, `.find` is one invocation = 2, `sameCells` = 2, total = 4)\n- Conditionals: `? :` ternary = 4\n- Loops: `find` = 5\n- Assignments: `match = ...` = 6\n\nTotal: 2 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 22\n\nPlus the `SUCCESSORS` constant definition:\n- Bindings: `SUCCESSORS` = 1\n- Constants: the array entries reference existing constants, so `L_SHAPE, BLOCK, BLOCK, VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER` — these are bindings (references), 5 of them = 5\n- Assignment: `SUCCESSORS = ...` = 6\n\nSo SUCCESSORS mass: 1 + 5 + 6 = 12\n\nHmm, so the refactored total is higher (22 + 12 = 34 vs 20). But APP mass isn't everything — Rule 2 (clarity) and Rule 3 (DRY) trump mass.\n\nWait, but is the lookup table actually clearer? Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I'm worried the lookup table is premature because the next tests will need a general algorithm. Let me think about whether there's a simpler, more targeted refactoring.\n\nHmm. Actually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The hardcoded-case approach is fundamentally a \"fake\" implementation that will be replaced once more tests arrive. The question is: what refactoring improves the CURRENT code without over-committing to a structure that's about to be thrown away?\n\nOptions:\n1. **Lookup table** — removes duplication, but adds a data structure that will be thrown away.\n2. **Extract the pattern matching into a helper** — marginal benefit.\n3. **Improve naming** — `sameCells` is fine; `nextGeneration` is fine.\n4. **Improve the comment / structure** — minor.\n\nLet me reconsider. The most valuable refactoring here, given that a general algorithm is coming, might actually be to START moving toward the general algorithm. But that's risky — I might break tests or implement ahead.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the situation. The current tests are:\n1. `[] -> []` ✓\n2. `[(0,0)] -> []` (underpopulation, 0 neighbors) ✓\n3. `[(0,1),(1,1)] -> []` (underpopulation, 1 neighbor each) ✓\n4. L-shape `[(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)] -> block` (reproduction) ✓\n5. block `[(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)] -> block` (still life) ✓\n6. vertical blinker `[(0,0),(0,1),(0,2)] -> horizontal` (oscillator) ✓\n\nTests 1, 2, 3 all return `[]`. The current code returns `[]` as the default/fallthrough. So those work via the default `return []`.\n\nTests 4, 5, 6 are hardcoded cases.\n\nNow, is it safe to implement a general Game of Life algorithm? Let me check each test against the actual rules:\n\nGame of Life rules:\n- A live cell with 0 or 1 neighbors dies (underpopulation)\n- A live cell with 2 or 3 neighbors survives\n- A live cell with 4+ neighbors dies (overpopulation)\n- A dead cell with exactly 3 neighbors becomes alive (reproduction)\n\nTest 1: `[] -> []`. Empty input → empty output. ✓ (no live cells)\nTest 2: `[(0,0)] -> []`. Single cell, 0 neighbors → dies. ✓\nTest 3: `[(0,1),(1,1)] -> []`. Each cell has 1 neighbor → both die. ✓\nTest 4: L-shape `[(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)] -> block [(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)]`. \n  - Live cells: (0,0) has 2 neighbors [(1,0),(0,1)] → survives. (1,0) has 2 neighbors [(0,0),(0,1)] → survives. (0,1) has 2 neighbors [(0,0),(1,0)] → survives.\n  - Dead cells with 3 neighbors: (1,1) has neighbors (0,0)? No wait. (1,1) neighbors: (0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(2,0),(2,1),(2,2),(1,2),(0,2). Live ones: (1,0),(0,1). That's 2, not 3. Hmm.\n  \n  Wait, let me recompute. L-shape is `[(0,0),(1,0),(0,1)]`. So live cells are (0,0), (1,0), (0,1).\n  - (1,1) neighbors: (0,0)✓, (0,1)✓, (1,0)✓, (2,0), (2,1), (2,2), (1,2), (0,2). Live: (0,0),(0,1),(1,0) = 3 neighbors! So (1,1) is born. ✓\n  - So the block is correct: (0,0),(1,0),(0,1) survive, (1,1) is born. = block. ✓\n\nTest 5: block `[(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)] -> block`. Each cell has 3 neighbors → survives. No dead cell has exactly 3 neighbors (each dead cell adjacent to the block has at most 2). So block stays. ✓\n\nTest 6: vertical blinker `[(0,0),(0,1),(0,2)] -> horizontal [(-1,1),(0,1),(1,1)]`.\n  - (0,1) has 2 neighbors [(0,0),(0,2)] → survives.\n  - (0,0) has 1 neighbor → dies.\n  - (0,2) has 1 neighbor → dies.\n  - Dead cells with 3 neighbors: (-1,1) neighbors: (0,0),(0,1),(0,2),(-1,0),(-2,0),(-2,1),(-2,2),(-1,2). Live: (0,0),(0,1),(0,2) = 3! Born. (1,1) similarly has 3 live neighbors. Born.\n  - So result: (0,1),(-1,1),(1,1) = horizontal blinker. ✓\n\nSo ALL six passing tests are consistent with the general Game of Life algorithm! This means I could potentially implement the general algorithm and all current tests would still pass.\n\nHmm, but the AGENTS.md says for the refactor phase: \"Run autonomously, return when done.\" and the refactor agent \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring (or document why none is possible)\". \n\nBut wait — implementing the general algorithm would be a big change. Is that \"refactoring\"? Refactoring is supposed to change internal structure without changing behavior. Implementing the general algorithm changes behavior (for inputs not yet tested), but for the TESTED inputs, the behavior is the same. Since all tests pass, it's a behavior-preserving change from the tests' perspective.\n\nBut hold on — is it wise for the refactor agent to implement the general algorithm? The whole TDD philosophy is to implement minimally and let tests drive the design. If I implement the general algorithm now, I'd be implementing ahead of the tests, which violates TDD. The next tests (overpopulation, negative coords, oscillator cycle) are precisely the tests that would drive the general algorithm.\n\nThe refactor agent's job is to improve code quality WITHOUT implementing new features ahead of tests. Implementing the general algorithm now would be \"implementing beyond what tests demand\" — a common TDD failure mode mentioned in the AGENTS.md.\n\nSo I should NOT implement the general algorithm. Instead, I should refactor the existing hardcoded-cases structure for clarity and DRY.\n\nGiven that, the lookup table is the most natural refactoring. It:\n- Eliminates the duplicated `sameCells(cells, X) → return Y` pattern (Rule 3)\n- Makes the data-driven nature explicit (Rule 2)\n- Makes adding future cases trivial (though we won't add them — that's for green phase)\n\nBut the lookup table adds mass. Let me reconsider whether it's actually clearer.\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider. With only 3 cases, the if-statements are quite readable. The lookup table adds a level of indirection. Is the duplication \"obvious\" or just \"conceptual\"?\n\nThe AGENTS.md says: \"Look for obvious and conceptual duplication.\" and \"Balance with Rule 2: If DRY hurts clarity, choose clarity.\"\n\nThe duplication here is the repeated `sameCells(cells, PATTERN)` check. It's fairly obvious. Three separate if-branches each doing a pattern match is a classic case where a lookup table helps.\n\nLet me go with the lookup table. It's the standard refactoring for \"a series of if-returns checking equality against known values.\" It clearly reveals intent: \"here are the known patterns and their successors; find the matching one.\"\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. I want to make sure the lookup table is genuinely better and not just different.\n\nCurrent:\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE) || sameCells(cells, BLOCK)) {\n    return BLOCK;\n  }\n  if (sameCells(cells, VERTICAL_BLINKER)) {\n    return HORIZONTAL_BLINKER;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nThis has a subtle conceptual issue: L_SHAPE and BLOCK both map to BLOCK, and they're OR'd. This obscures the fact that there are really three (pattern → successor) mappings: L_SHAPE→BLOCK, BLOCK→BLOCK, VERTICAL_BLINKER→HORIZONTAL_BLINKER. The OR-combining hides that L_SHAPE→BLOCK is a distinct mapping from BLOCK→BLOCK.\n\nLookup table:\n```typescript\nconst SUCCESSORS: Array<[Cell[], Cell[]]> = [\n  [L_SHAPE, BLOCK],\n  [BLOCK, BLOCK],\n  [VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER],\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const match = SUCCESSORS.find(([pattern]) => sameCells(cells, pattern));\n  return match ? match[1] : [];\n}\n```\n\nThis makes the three mappings explicit and uniform. Each row is a (pattern, successor) pair. This is clearer and removes duplication.\n\nI think this is a good refactoring. Let me go with it.\n\nBut wait, let me reconsider the mass trade-off once more, carefully, including all the constants and the lookup table definition, to be honest about APP.\n\nActually, the constants `L_SHAPE`, `BLOCK`, etc. and `sameCells` are unchanged. So the mass delta is only in:\n- Removing the old `nextGeneration` body\n- Adding `SUCCESSORS` constant + new `nextGeneration` body\n\nLet me compute both precisely.\n\n**Old `nextGeneration` body mass:**\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE) || sameCells(cells, BLOCK)) {\n    return BLOCK;\n  }\n  if (sameCells(cells, VERTICAL_BLINKER)) {\n    return HORIZONTAL_BLINKER;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nComponents:\n- Bindings (params + locals): `cells` = 1\n- Constants: `[]` (the `return []`) = 1. (The constant identifiers L_SHAPE, BLOCK, etc. are bindings/references, not literal constants. Hmm, actually in APP, named constants... are they constants or bindings? A named const is a binding that holds a value. References to it are bindings. I'll count references to named constants as bindings.)\n  \n  Let me reconsider. In APP:\n  - Constant (mass 1): Literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`). Also `[]` and `{}` are literal values.\n  - Binding/Scalar (mass 1): Variables, parameters.\n  \n  Named constants like `L_SHAPE` — references to them are bindings (mass 1 each). The `[]` empty array literal is a constant (mass 1). `false`, `true` are constants.\n\nOK here's my count for old `nextGeneration`:\n- Bindings: `cells` (param) + references `L_SHAPE`, `BLOCK`, `BLOCK`, `VERTICAL_BLINKER`, `HORIZONTAL_BLINKER` = 6 bindings\n- Constants: `[]` = 1\n- Invocations: `sameCells(...)` ×3 = 6\n- Conditionals: `if` (4) + `||` (4) + `if` (4) = 12\n- Loops: 0\n- Assignments: 0\n\nTotal old: 6 + 1 + 6 + 12 = 25\n\n**New code mass (SUCCESSORS + new nextGeneration):**\n\n```typescript\nconst SUCCESSORS: Array<[Cell[], Cell[]]> = [\n  [L_SHAPE, BLOCK],\n  [BLOCK, BLOCK],\n  [VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER],\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const match = SUCCESSORS.find(([pattern]) => sameCells(cells, pattern));\n  return match ? match[1] : [];\n}\n```\n\nSUCCESSORS definition:\n- Bindings: `SUCCESSORS` (the name) + references `L_SHAPE`, `BLOCK`, `BLOCK`, `VERTICAL_BLINKER`, `HORIZONTAL_BLINKER` = 6\n- Constants: 0 (no literals; the arrays contain references)\n- Assignments: `SUCCESSORS = [...]` = 6\n- (The array literal `[]` outer is a constant = 1? An array literal is a constant value. Hmm. I'll count the outer `[...]` as a constant = 1. And inner tuples... each `[a, b]` is an array literal. This gets complicated. Let me be a bit lenient and count the outer array structure as constant mass. Actually, to be consistent, if I count `[]` in the old code as a constant, I should count array literals here as constants too.)\n\nHmm, this is getting into the weeds. Let me simplify and just count the meaningful differences. The array literals are a wash conceptually.\n\nLet me just count reasonably:\n\nSUCCESSORS:\n- Binding `SUCCESSORS` = 1\n- 5 referenced constants (L_SHAPE, BLOCK×2, VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER) = 5\n- Assignment `=` = 6\n- (array literal structures — let's say a few constants, ~3) = 3\n\nSUCCESSORS subtotal ≈ 1 + 5 + 6 + 3 = 15\n\nnew nextGeneration:\n- Bindings: `cells` (param), `match`, `pattern` (destructured param), and references `SUCCESSORS`, `match`, `match`, `cells`, `pattern`, `sameCells`... \n  Let me list: `cells` param (1), `match` (1), inside callback `pattern` (1). References: `SUCCESSORS` (1), `cells` (1), `pattern` (1), `match` (1, in `match ?`), `match` (1, in `match[1]`). \n  Bindings total: cells + match + pattern + SUCCESSORS + cells + pattern + match + match = 8\n- Constants: `[]` (1)\n- Invocations: `SUCCESSORS.find(...)` (2), `sameCells(...)` (2) = 4\n- Conditionals: ternary `? :` = 4\n- Loops: `find` = 5\n- Assignments: `match = ...` = 6\n\nnew nextGeneration subtotal: 8 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 5 + 6 = 28\n\nNew total: 15 + 28 = 43\n\nOld total: 25\n\nSo new mass (43) > old mass (25). The mass went UP by ~18.\n\nHmm. That's a significant mass increase. But APP mass is the LOWEST priority (Rule 4). Rule 2 (clarity) and Rule 3 (DRY) trump Rule 4.\n\nBut is the lookup table actually clearer (Rule 2)? And is the duplication bad enough to justify the added mass and indirection?\n\nLet me reconsider. I'm now doubting the lookup table. The added mass and indirection might not be worth it for 3 cases that are about to be replaced by a general algorithm anyway.\n\nLet me think about what's the MINIMAL, most defensible refactoring.\n\nActually, maybe the best refactoring is a simpler one: the three if-branches are fine, but I can improve clarity. Let me look again:\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  // The L-shape reproduces into the block; the block itself is a still life.\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE) || sameCells(cells, BLOCK)) {\n    return BLOCK;\n  }\n  if (sameCells(cells, VERTICAL_BLINKER)) {\n    return HORIZONTAL_BLINKER;\n  }\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nThe comment is a bit odd — it explains L-shape and block but not the blinker. \n\nHmm, what about a different approach: extract a helper that finds the successor of a known pattern. This is essentially the lookup table but framed as a function:\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what refactoring is genuinely justified here.\n\nThe strongest case for refactoring is the conceptual duplication: three \"pattern → successor\" mappings encoded as imperative if-branches. The lookup table is the canonical refactoring for this.\n\nBut given:\n1. The mass increases significantly\n2. The structure is about to be replaced by a general algorithm (next tests will force it)\n3. Only 3 cases\n\n...I'm torn. Let me reconsider whether the lookup table is genuinely clearer.\n\nActually, I think the lookup table IS clearer and more intention-revealing. It explicitly says \"here are the known (pattern, successor) pairs.\" The current code hides this structure in control flow. The mass increase is acceptable because Rule 2 and Rule 3 trump Rule 4.\n\nBut wait, am I over-engineering? Rule 4 says \"Fewest Elements\" — don't add unnecessary abstractions. Is the lookup table an unnecessary abstraction? With 3 cases, a lookup table is borderline. But it's a very standard, simple data structure, not a heavy abstraction.\n\nLet me make a decision. I'll go with the lookup table because:\n1. It removes clear duplication (Rule 3) — the repeated `sameCells(cells, X)` pattern\n2. It reveals intent better (Rule 2) — the (pattern → successor) mappings become explicit, uniform data\n3. It makes the code data-driven, which is easier to understand and extend\n\nThe mass increase is acceptable since Rule 2/3 trump Rule 4.\n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider one more time. Let me re-read the guideline: \"Balance with Rule 2: If DRY hurts clarity, choose clarity.\" and \"Lower mass = Better code (generally)\" and \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass.\"\n\nI think the lookup table is a net improvement in clarity AND removes duplication. The mass increase is the cost, but it's justified.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether there's an even simpler refactoring that reduces mass AND improves clarity.\n\nWhat if I just keep the structure but the issue is conceptual duplication is mild here. Let me look at it from the angle of \"what's the single most valuable improvement.\"\n\nActually, I realize I should consider: is the `||` combining L_SHAPE and BLOCK actually a clarity problem? It conflates two distinct mappings (L_SHAPE→BLOCK and BLOCK→BLOCK) into one branch. Separating them would be clearer:\n\n```typescript\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  if (sameCells(cells, L_SHAPE)) return BLOCK;\n  if (sameCells(cells, BLOCK)) return BLOCK;\n  if (sameCells(cells, VERTICAL_BLINKER)) return HORIZONTAL_BLINKER;\n  return [];\n}\n```\n\nThis is clearer (each mapping on its own line, uniform structure) but has MORE duplication (sameCells called 3 times with the same first arg, `return BLOCK` twice). Mass goes up slightly (extra conditional + invocation vs the `||`).\n\nThis doesn't reduce duplication; it increases it. So not great alone.\n\nOK, let me commit to the lookup table approach. It's the right call for removing the conceptual duplication while keeping clarity.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider the mass calculation because I might be over-counting the new code. The destructuring `([pattern]) =>` — `pattern` is a binding. `match[1]` accesses index 1 — is `1` a constant? Yes, index `1` is a literal constant (mass 1). Let me add that.\n\nAlso `[pattern]` destructuring might count as an assignment? Destructuring binds a value. I'll count `pattern` as a binding (1) and not double-count.\n\nThis is getting too deep. The point is: mass goes up, but clarity/DRY improve. Let me just present a reasonable mass calculation and proceed.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider the whole thing ONE more time, because I want to make a good decision, not just any decision.\n\nThe core tension: \n- The current code works and is fairly readable.\n- There's mild duplication (3 sameCells checks).\n- A general algorithm is coming that will replace all of this.\n\nGiven a general algorithm is imminent, the BEST refactoring might be one that doesn't over-invest in the lookup-table structure. \n\nBut the rules REQUIRE me to attempt at least one refactoring. And the lookup table is a legitimate, standard refactoring that improves the current code. Even if it's temporary, it makes the current code cleaner. And honestly, when the general algorithm comes, the developer can decide whether to keep the lookup table as an optimization/fast-path or replace it entirely.\n\nDecision: Go with the lookup table. It's the most defensible refactoring: removes duplication, improves clarity, data-driven.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider whether to make the change even smaller/safer. What if I'm wrong that all tests pass with the lookup table? Let me trace through:\n\nTest 1: `nextGeneration([])`. `SUCCESSORS.find(...)` — none match (empty cells won't match L_SHAPE etc. because lengths differ). `match` is `undefined`. Return `[]`. ✓\nTest 2: `nextGeneration([[0,0]])`. No pattern matches (length 1 vs length 3/4). Return `[]`. ✓\nTest 3: `nextGeneration([[0,1],[1,1]])`. No pattern matches (length 2). Return `[]`. ✓\nTest 4: `nextGeneration([[0,0],[1,0],[0,1]])` = L_SHAPE. `find` returns `[L_SHAPE, BLOCK]`. `match[1]` = BLOCK. ✓\nTest 5: `nextGeneration([[0,0],[1,0],[0,1],[1,1]])` = BLOCK. `find` — first checks L_SHAPE (length 3 vs 4, no), then BLOCK (matches). Returns `[BLOCK, BLOCK]`. `match[1]` = BLOCK. ✓\nTest 6: `nextGeneration([[0,0],[0,1],[0,2]])` = VERTICAL_BLINKER. `find` checks L_SHAPE (no), BLOCK (no), VERTICAL_BLINKER (yes). Returns `[VERTICAL_BLINKER, HORIZONTAL_BLINKER]`. `match[1]` = HORIZONTAL_BLINKER. ✓\n\nAll pass. Good.\n\nNow let me write the refactored code.\n\nActually, I realize there might be an even cleaner way to write the lookup with better naming. Let me think about names:\n- `SUCCESSORS` — good, describes what it is (mapping of pattern to successor).\n- `match` — okay, but could be more descriptive. `successor`? But it's the matched entry, not just the successor. Hmm. Actually since we return `match[1]`, naming it `match` is fine. Or I could destructure differently.\n\nAlternative using `.find` returning the successor directly via the entry:\n\n```typescript\nconst KNOWN_SUCCESSIONS: ReadonlyArray<{ pattern: Cell[]; successor: Cell[] }> = [\n  { pattern: L_SHAPE, successor: BLOCK },\n  { pattern: BLOCK, successor: BLOCK },\n  { pattern: VERTICAL_BLINKER, successor: HORIZONTAL_BLINKER },\n];\n\nexport function nextGeneration(cells: Cell[]): Cell[] {\n  const succession = KNOWN_SUCCESSIONS.find(({ pattern }) => sameCells(cells, pattern));\n  return succession?.successor ?? [];\n}\n```\n\nThis uses named fields (`pattern`, `successor`) which is clearer than tuple indexing `match[1]`. But it adds object literal syntax. Hmm. Named fields improve clarity (Rule 2) at the cost of more mass.\n\nActually, I think named fields (`pattern`, `successor`) are clearer than `[pattern]` destructuring and `match[1]` indexing. Let me go with named fields. The clarity gain is worth it.\n\nBut wait — `?.` and `??` are conditionals? Optional chaining and nullish coalescing. They're